[center][size=200][b]UTTchain[/b][/size]
[size=120]Unified Trading Terminal • Robinhood Chain Protocol Layer[/size][/center]

[b]UTTchain[/b] is a new protocol and infrastructure project being developed for the [b]Unified Trading Terminal (UTT)[/b] and [b]Unified Trading Terminal Token (UTTT)[/b], built around Robinhood Chain.

The goal is not to force an entire trading terminal into smart contracts. UTTchain is being designed as a hybrid system in which the web terminal, market-data services, accounting, exchange integrations, databases, and private user state remain off-chain where appropriate, while Robinhood Chain provides a verifiable protocol layer for wallet-authorized execution, canonical identity, settlement, deployment identity, selected state commitments, and UTTT infrastructure.

[b]Current status[/b]

UTTchain is in its [b]foundation / architecture stage[/b].

[list]
[*]No UTTchain protocol is live.
[*]No UTTT migration is authorized.
[*]No new Robinhood Chain UTTT deployment has been designated as the canonical supply.
[*]No liquidity or market action is authorized by the project repository.
[/list]

[b]Relationship to Unified Trading Terminal[/b]

UTT already exists as a local-first multi-venue trading terminal with a React frontend and FastAPI backend. UTTchain is intended to extend UTT toward a web-accessible and wallet-native architecture without discarding the capabilities that belong off-chain.

[code]
Unified Trading Terminal
          |
   +------+------+
   |             |
Application    Protocol
 Layer          Layer
   |             |
React/FastAPI  UTTchain
                 |
          Robinhood Chain
[/code]

The existing terminal remains responsible for workloads such as CEX APIs, market aggregation, relational accounting, FIFO/cost-basis logic, indexing, caching, and private application state.

UTTchain will focus on blockchain responsibilities that genuinely benefit from public verification.

[b]UTTT and one canonical economic supply[/b]

UTTT already exists in multiple ecosystems, including Robinhood Chain, Solana, the Polkadot ecosystem, and Counterparty.

Those existing assets will not be casually discarded. Before a canonical supply model is selected, the project will reconcile each deployment or representation for:

[list]
[*]identifier/address
[*]issuer or deployer
[*]current and historical supply
[*]burns
[*]holder distribution
[*]treasury balances
[*]liquidity and market pairs
[*]bridge or migration history
[*]administrative authority
[/list]

The design objective is:

[code]
ONE UTTT
ONE ECONOMIC SUPPLY
MULTIPLE POSSIBLE EXECUTION DOMAINS
[/code]

The project will not simply add nominal supplies across chains together. Burned, bridged, escrowed, mirrored, or otherwise non-independent units must be identified so UTTT is not double counted.

Possible future models include lock-and-mint representations, a canonical supply ledger spanning existing assets, deterministic migration, or a hybrid model that preserves economically meaningful legacy markets. No model has been selected yet.

[b]Planned UTTchain components[/b]

[list]
[*][b]UTTT canonicalization[/b] — forensic review of existing UTTT deployments before any replacement or migration decision.
[*][b]UTTchain Registry[/b] — compact on-chain authority for canonical deployment, chain, contract, and protocol-component identities.
[*][b]Deployment manifests[/b] — machine-readable deployment evidence tied to source, compiler, chain, runtime identity, and verification state.
[*][b]UTT integration adapter[/b] — a clean interface between the existing terminal and UTTchain contracts/manifests.
[*][b]Wallet-native execution[/b] — browser-wallet authorization rather than hidden backend signing.
[*][b]Future account abstraction[/b] — research into ERC-4337 smart accounts, session keys, batching, and bounded permissions after ordinary wallet execution is stable.
[*][b]Future state commitments[/b] — cryptographic proofs or roots for selected application/accounting state without publicly exposing the underlying private records.
[/list]

[b]Security principles[/b]

[list]
[*]No private keys or seed phrases in GitHub.
[*]No production exchange secrets in GitHub or frontend bundles.
[*]No CEX API credentials on-chain.
[*]No automatic blockchain signing by the backend.
[*]No unlimited token approvals by default.
[*]No ticker-only asset authority.
[*]No silent mainnet broadcasts.
[*]No undocumented privileged contract roles.
[*]No production deployment from unreconciled source.
[*]No UTTT holder migration before multi-chain supply and ownership reconciliation.
[/list]

[b]Development roadmap[/b]

[code]
Repository + security foundation
        |
Architecture / threat model
        |
Multi-chain UTTT forensic intake
        |
Canonical-supply design
        |
Contract specification / testing
        |
UTTchain Registry + manifests
        |
Robinhood Chain testnet
        |
UTT integration
        |
Web-service + multi-user security
        |
Wallet-native execution
        |
Production security review
        |
Explicit mainnet authorization
[/code]

A deployed contract is not automatically an activated protocol, and a website being online does not automatically authorize production trading.

